    "extensionPanels": "gpui-shell",
    "agentPanels": "in_process",
    "webView": false,
    "text": "gpui-kit: the two-day spike passed every must-have on the reference MacBook Air (Mac15,12, M3, 16 GB, macOS 23H723) on October 20, 2026. The window with its 1,000-row session list renders and is clicked headlessly; the menu-bar count, 143 waiting, is an AppKit status item that GPUI's offscreen renderer can't capture, checked by its title against the window's count. Both agents' JavaScript stocks panels, written from gpui-shell's docs and the brief alone, load through gpui-shell and pass the headless test, Codex's on round 0 and Claude Code's after one fix, the stocks panel runs on recorded quotes, and the app's report, listing the recorded quotes, opens in the browser. Worst of five runs after warm-up: cold start 204.7 ms, idle CPU 0.05%, memory 51.6 MB, the stripped app 49.8 MB, and the local preflight with its snapshot renders 2.8 min against 1.1 min at the spike branch's fork point from main, 0c5e145; a clean release build took 4.4 min. A budget counts only with the thermal state nominal: the fanless Air reaches fair after about three back-to-back preflights, and a throttled run took up to 7.0 min, so the driver idled until nominal and three minutes longer before each counted run. Both agents' panels ran in process through gpui-shell on the UI thread: Codex's in the release app, where gpui-shell's JIT is on, and both in the debug headless tests, which stay on the interpreter. The panel host isn't tested by this spike; it lands in R2. gpui-shell is at its first milestone and not yet a stable interface; extension panels run in it, with its registry's coverage of the app checked by #338, and the app has no web view: web output opens in your browser."
  },
  "findings": [
    "Both agents wrote their panels from gpui-shell's docs and the brief alone, with no generated type declarations: Codex passed on round 0, Claude Code after one fix.",
    "Claude Code's chart is a row of dots, not a line chart; the headless test checks texts and clicks, not the chart's form.",
    "gpui-shell writes gpui-kit.d.ts and jsconfig.json into a panel's directory each time it loads it.",
    "gpui-shell's release builds JIT hot functions to native code with no switch; debug builds, so the tests, stay on the interpreter.",
    "Codex's panel keeps one alert on screen: a later symbol's crossing replaces an earlier one, where the brief says each alert stays shown. The headless test arms one symbol, so it passes; a test that arms two would catch it.",
    "A binary's first launch after it is built is slower, since macOS checks a new binary once: 1,198.7 ms before the abandoned app measurement, the first launch after the build, and 853.4 and 1,008.2 ms in the draft record's runs on this Mac, against cold starts of 118.5-204.7 ms after it; the counted measurement's untimed launch of the already-checked binary took 381.3 ms 34 minutes later, with the Node 22 preflight series having run in between. A user pays the check once after installing; the budget runs follow one untimed launch.",
    "The fanless MacBook Air reaches the fair thermal state after about three back-to-back preflights. Perf then judges its budgets INCONCLUSIVE, and the preflight slows: in an earlier series on October 20 two throttled runs took 320.6 s and 420.2 s, with the check lanes at 41 s of each and the rest in perf. The counted runs each began and ended nominal after the machine idled three minutes past nominal.",
    "The spike's base, 0c5e145, is the spike branch's fork point from main, not the commit before the app landed (#599 merged onto 0c024ce, after #592, #590 and #598) and not this branch's merge base with main (f346706, which already holds the app). So the preflight comparison, 1.1 min to 2.8 min, carries #592, #590 and #598 as well as the app and its headless renders. The check lanes go from 12-13 s to 36-40 s, and perf from about 50 s (inferred from the base's wall time, since its perf lane fails and logs no time) to 117-127 s.",
    "The draft record's 6.9 min preflight, taken at 5b56adf before #594, had perf rebuilding the Rust tree cold in temporary copies on every run; with #594 keeping each side's Cargo builds between runs, the same preflight reads 2.8 min here. The preflight once read over the limit on this Mac for that reason, not the app's."
  ]
